<section class="specialized-notes" aria-labelledby="image-notes-title">
  <header><span class="section-index">AFTER THE REPORT</span><h2 id="image-notes-title">What image metadata can tell you</h2><p>The report is the useful part. These two notes explain its boundaries.</p></header>
  <div class="specialized-note-list">
    <details>
      <summary>What the viewer reads</summary>
      <div><p>JPEG, PNG, and WebP can carry camera settings, GPS, author details, software names, color profiles, thumbnails, XMP, IPTC, and application-specific text.</p><p>PNG text chunks and WebP containers are checked alongside EXIF. Unknown readable tags stay available in the complete field view.</p></div>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>What metadata cannot prove</summary>
      <div><p>Fields can be edited, removed, or wrong. A missing location does not prove that a photo never had one, and a camera name is not cryptographic evidence.</p><p>Faces, signs, reflections, landmarks, and license plates are pixels. This viewer does not inspect them.</p></div>
    </details>
  </div>
</section>
